CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe autonomic crisis, including intractable vomiting, marked tachycardia (HR 115), and severe hyperventilation leading to painful carpopedal spasms, driven entirely by internalized psychological trauma. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to perform an abdominal examination to definitively exclude an acute surgical abdomen, manually break the hyperventilation cycle to resolve the spasms, and administer an antiemetic to prevent severe dehydration. Delaying assessment until the morning would have perpetuated severe physical distress and risked significant metabolic derangement, fulfilling the criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ondansetron 4mg to immediately halt the intractable vomiting. Guided the patient through targeted box-breathing exercises to correct the respiratory alkalosis and resolve the carpopedal spasms. Recommended the usual GP assess baseline sympathetic arousal and administer the Internalized Homonegativity Inventory.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute somatic panic, implement the "ice water dive" technique: submerge the face in a bowl of ice water for 15 seconds during severe distress; this triggers the mammalian dive reflex, rapidly and safely lowering the heart rate. To address mannerism policing, practice a 5-minute "unmasking exercise" daily in front of a mirror in a completely private, locked room; allowing oneself to safely exhibit natural mannerisms in a controlled environment slowly reduces the internal shame response.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an LGBTQIA+ affirmative psychologist.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for a comprehensive psychological assessment and referral coordination.

